s://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42848","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42848"}},{"id":"42851","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"The big drop.  We ran the tongue right into the big wave, then drove hard right (or tried) to avoid a line of rocks. The left side has a hole  that looked a little sticky. The next day the level was 2' higher (on the Black) and everyone agreed the hole should be avoided.","caption":"Drop","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42851","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42851"}},{"id":"42855","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"The bottom of the last drop.","caption":"The last drop","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42855","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42855"}},{"id":"42850","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"Lead in to the big drop.","caption":"Lead in","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42850","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42850"}},{"id":"42849","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"After about 2 1/2 miles the gradient starts and things pick up.","caption":"The gradient starts","poi_name":"","subject":"Hanno, Dave, Cameron","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42849","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42849"}},{"id":"42854","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"The top and river left side of the last drop.","caption":"Last drop","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42854","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42854"}},{"id":"42847","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9044","description":"The very convenient pool for putting in. ","caption":"Little Woodhull put in","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Steve McLuckie","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42847","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42847"}}],"detail":{"id":"1340","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This creek has two sections:\n\n**Stone Dam Trail to Dam at Kincaid Road**\n\nFirst section is from 'Stone Dam Trail' (off North Lake Road) to the dam on Kincaid road. There is a little reservoir above this dam. River miles about 7 miles, (6 on the creek and 1 on the reservoir.) Looking at the topo map, it's flat the first four miles, then gets some gradient, then of course flat again on the reservoir. 8.7 driving miles one-way. There might be access on some of the smaller roads.\n\n**Dam on Kincaid Road to Woodhull River Confluence**\n\nThis is a nice section. It starts with 100 yards of rock garden, then 2.0 miles of very flat, windy water with great views. At 2.0 miles, the water starts to move. About a half mile later you will get your first significant drop. Scout all horizon lines from this point on.\n\nWhen you get to the last drop (2.9 m) above the bridge (3.0 m), we run it on the right, *directly* into the roostertail at the bottom; it sends you airborne but be careful of hanging up on the boulders 10 yards down.\n\nThere is still a big slide (3.1 m) after the bridge. The left is safe but a bumpy ride at lower levels. The right is safe too but wilder, at really high levels watch out. Be careful of a 8-inch piece of rebar/pipe embedded in the rock at the center top of this section. It is visible at lower levers (7 blocks) but not higher levels (5.5 blocks).\n\nThe confluence with the Woodhull (~3.3 m) is ahead, below the Woodhull's put-in rapid. \\[maps.goople.com terrain view mistakenly calls the Woodhull 'Black Creek'\\]\n\n2.8 driving miles one-way if you takeout at the Woodhull takeout lot.","averageGradient":"0","maxGradient":"0","length":"9.1","correlations":[{"reachID":"1340","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"6","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"12"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"BLACK RIVER NEAR BOONVILLE NY","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"04252500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"821","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"5.17","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"43.5116111","longitude":"-75.30677778"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/04252500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/04252500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nRange of Boatable Flows (R0-R9): 6 - 12 levelFT  Comment: Check the bridge at the takeout to be sure this is running. Read the description for more info.\n\nMigration Errors: \nSingle range is valid but doesn't provide low/medium/high runnable\nThere is one comment for all of low/medium/high runnable.","comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"5.17","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","ad